{
  "raw": {
    "https://ontariotechu.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/2273?c=ontariotechu": {
      "title": "Customer Service Coordinator",
      "description": "Customer Service Coordinator | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Customer Service Coordinator Oshawa, ON, Canada | req2273 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/15/2026 Back to Search Customer Service Coordinator Tracking Code: req2273 Faculty/Department: Office of Ancillary Services Number of Positions: 2 Appointment Type: Full-Time Continuing Hours of Work: 35 hours per week Salary Range: Level 7 - Starting Salary, Step 1 $70,997 Posting Date: October 15, 2026 Closing Date: October 21, 2026 (11:59 pm EST) Job Summary: Parking Services is a joint service supporting both Ontario Tech University and Durham College across multiple locations, including the shared North Oshawa campus, Ontario Tech’s downtown Oshawa campus, and Durham College’s Whitby campus. The Parking Services team works across both institutions to provide consistent, customer-focused parking services to students, employees, visitors and the broader campus community. As a first point of contact for Parking Services, UCard and UPass, you will help students, employees and visitors navigate a range of campus services. This is a busy, front-line position that combines customer service, administrative processing, technology and systems support, and day-to-day problem solving. The successful candidate will be comfortable working in a fast-paced environment, responding to a high volume of inquiries and resolving conflicts with professionalism, good judgement and empathy. This position is an on-campus, in-person role. The successful candidate will be required to work between all three campus locations and must arrange their own transportation. While the majority of the work will take place weekdays between 8:30 a.m. and 4:30 p.m., occasional evenings and weekends will be required to support peak periods and special events. Responsibilities/Accountabilities: In this role, you will: Provide front-line customer service in person, by phone and by email for parking, UCard and UPass inquiries. Support day-to-day UCard and UPass operations, including card production, distribution, troubleshooting and system support. Process parking permits, payments, fines and fees, and maintain accurate customer and transaction records. Set up and administer parking arrangements for visitors and special events. Help customers understand parking policies, permits, payment options and UCard accounts. Provide first-level support for parking-related complaints and citation appeals. Troubleshoot basic issues involving parking equipment, payment kiosks, UCard/UPass technology, permits and account systems. Coordinate with vendors and technical support teams to resolve equipment and system issues. Maintain accurate records and contribute to operational reporting, KPI tracking and audit controls. Support parking operations during special events and peak periods, including assisting customers in parking areas and setting up signage, cones and barricades. Participate in team meetings, training, operational planning and continuous improvement initiatives. Required Education Completion of a two-year post-secondary diploma; a program related to business, information technology, or hospitality is required. A bachelor's degree is considered an asset. One to three years of experience in a high-volume and/or high-conflict customer service or enforcement / compliance environment. A combination of education and experience may be considered for this role. Required Experience: Minimum 1 year of experience is required. Experience with campus card and parking administration systems is required. Required Skills: The following technical skills are required: - Proficient with Microsoft Office and Google Workspace, Banner, IBM Cognos Analytics, TouchNet OneCard VIP, CloudCard, Presto portal, Cascade Content Management Systems and Mach Forms, FAST, Concur, OpsComm, HONK, Parknet Parking Management System hardware, Logic Control Parking Management software. You are someone who: Delivers excellent customer service and demonstrates sound judgement, flexibility and adaptability. Demonstrated the ability to de-escalate contentious situations. Can remain tactful and composed while working in a busy, high-pressure environment. Communicates clearly and professionally, both verbally and in writing. Has strong troubleshooting, problem-solving and critical-thinking skills. Pays close attention to detail and accuracy. Approaches customers with empathy, patience and professionalism. Takes initiative and can work both independently and as part of a collaborative team. Is highly organized and able to prioritize competing demands with a sense of urgency. Can build positive working relationships with a diverse range of students, employees, visitors and campus partners. Is comfortable learning and working with new technology and systems. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit in electronic format a covering letter and their resume. Applications will be accepted until October 21, 2026 or until a suitable candidate is found. We appreciate all applications received; however, only those candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. This position falls within the bargaining unit represented by the Ontario Public Service Employees Union (OPSEU) and will be subject to the terms and conditions of the collective agreement between the university and the OPSEU. To the extent that policies are not included in the collective agreement, employment will also be governed by the university’s policies which may also be found on our website https://ontariotechu.ca and which may be amended from time-to-time. Ontario\nTech University is actively committed to equity, diversity, inclusion, indigenization\nand decolonization (EDIID), and welcomes applications from all qualified\ncandidates, while especially encouraging applications from First Nations,\nMetis, Inuit peoples, Indigenous peoples of North America, racialized persons,\npersons with disabilities, and those who identify as women and/or\n2SLGBTQ+. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply;\nhowever, Canadian citizens, permanent residents, Indigenous Peoples in Canada, and those eligible to work in\nCanada, will be given priority. Ontario Tech University respects people's different needs and therefore will take all reasonable steps to ensure accommodation for applicants where appropriate. The University is also committed to ensuring that confidentiality is maintained throughout all aspects of the recruitment cycle. If you require accommodation, please contact Julie Day , Health and Disability Management Specialist. For more information about the universities policies for accommodating employees with disabilities please review the university’s Accessibility Policy The university acknowledges the lands and people of the Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nation which is covered under the Williams Treaties. We are situated on the Traditional Territory of the Mississaugas, a branch of the greater Anishinaabeg Nation which includes Algonquin, Ojibway, Odawa and Pottawatomi. Job Location: Oshawa, Ontario, Canada Expected Start Date: 11/1/2026 Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://ontariotechu.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/2269?c=ontariotechu": {
      "title": "Parking Compliance Officer",
      "description": "Parking Compliance Officer | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Parking Compliance Officer Oshawa, ON, Canada | req2269 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/15/2026 Back to Search Parking Compliance Officer Tracking Code: req2269 Faculty/Department: Office of Ancillary Services Number of Positions: 2 Appointment Type: Full-Time Continuing Hours of Work: 35 hours per week Salary Range: Level 5 - Starting Salary, Step 1 $59,797 Posting Date: October 15, 2026 Closing Date: October 21, 2026 (11:59 pm EST) Job Summary: Parking Services is a joint service supporting both Ontario Tech University and Durham College across multiple locations, including the shared North Oshawa campus, Ontario Tech’s downtown Oshawa campus, and Durham College’s Whitby campus. The Parking Services team works across both institutions to provide consistent, customer-focused parking services to students, employees, visitors and the broader campus community. The Parking Compliance Officer helps ensure the safe, orderly and effective use of campus parking facilities by monitoring parking areas, supporting compliance with parking regulations and payment requirements, and providing professional front-line assistance to customers. This is an active, field-based position that involves driving, walking and working outdoors throughout the year. Compliance Officers patrol parking facilities across the North Oshawa campus, Ontario Tech’s downtown Oshawa campus and Durham College’s Whitby campus. The successful candidate will be comfortable working independently, using technology in the field, applying policies consistently and interacting professionally with customers—including during difficult or potentially challenging situations. This position is an on-campus, in-person role. The successful candidate will be required to work between all three campus locations and must arrange their own transportation to and from work locations.  A mix of weekday, evening and weekend shifts will be required. Responsibilities/Accountabilities: In this role, you will: Conduct regular patrols of university and college parking facilities either on foot or using a vehicle equipped with Licence Plate Recognition (LPR) technology. Monitor compliance with parking permits, payments and regulations and issue parking violation notices or tow warnings when appropriate. Identify and document suspected fraudulent or stolen permits and other parking irregularities. Support vehicle immobilization and towing processes when required. Serve as a first point of contact for customers in parking areas, providing assistance with wayfinding, payment options, permits, parking citations, and immobilized or towed vehicles. Provide clear, respectful explanations of parking rules and violations and help customers resolve routine payment or permit issues. Use customer-service, conflict-resolution and de-escalation techniques when dealing with difficult interactions. Participate in the parking appeals process as required. Monitor parking equipment, signage, pay stations, LPR systems and other infrastructure and report operational or safety concerns. Perform basic troubleshooting of LPR equipment and other parking technology in the field and coordinate service or maintenance requests when needed. Maintain accurate activity logs, incident reports and other operational documentation. Assist with basic vehicle and equipment maintenance, including fueling, mileage tracking and monitoring supplies. Assist with traffic direction and vehicle flow during high-volume periods and campus events. Support temporary parking arrangements and respond to changing operational conditions during events. Working Environment: This is a field-based position that requires regular driving and LPR monitoring, as well as frequent walking, standing and entering and exiting a vehicle throughout the workday. Parking Compliance Officers work outdoors throughout the year and must be comfortable working in a variety of weather conditions, including periods of extreme heat and cold. The position may also involve moving signs or equipment and performing basic troubleshooting of equipment in the field. Because this is a front-line compliance role, the position also involves regular interaction with customers who may be upset or frustrated. The successful candidate must be able to remain calm, professional and respectful while explaining parking requirements and applying established policies consistently. Required Skills: The following technical skills are required: Proficient with Microsoft Office and Google Workspace, OpsComm, HONK, Parknet Parking Management System hardware, Logic Control Parking Management software. Experience using LPR Technology, SurVision and Zebra printers. You are someone who: Provides professional and respectful customer service, even in challenging situations. Has strong conflict-resolution and de-escalation skills. Can apply policies and procedures consistently and exercise sound judgement in the field. Is comfortable working independently and making decisions in real time. Has strong attention to detail and accuracy. Is comfortable using technology, including tablets, mobile phones, radios and parking-related systems. Has working knowledge of Microsoft Excel, Word and Outlook. Can accurately document incidents, observations and interactions. Is dependable, adaptable and able to respond to changing operational needs. Required Education: Completion of high school. Municipal Law Enforcement Officer (MLEO) certification is preferred. A combination of relevant education and experience may be considered. Required Experience: Up to one year of relevant experience is required. Previous experience in parking compliance is required. Experience using Licence Plate Recognition technology is required. Knowledge of parking bylaws and regulations is an asset. A valid Ontario Class G driver's licence with a clear driver's abstract is required. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit in electronic format a covering letter and their resume. Applications will be accepted until October 21, 2026 or until a suitable candidate is found. We appreciate all applications received; however, only those candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. This position falls within the bargaining unit represented by the Ontario Public Service Employees Union (OPSEU) and will be subject to the terms and conditions of the collective agreement between the university and the OPSEU. To the extent that policies are not included in the collective agreement, employment will also be governed by the university’s policies which may also be found on our website https://ontariotechu.ca and which may be amended from time-to-time. Ontario\nTech University is actively committed to equity, diversity, inclusion, indigenization\nand decolonization (EDIID), and welcomes applications from all qualified\ncandidates, while especially encouraging applications from First Nations,\nMetis, Inuit peoples, Indigenous peoples of North America, racialized persons,\npersons with disabilities, and those who identify as women and/or\n2SLGBTQ+. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply;\nhowever, Canadian citizens, permanent residents, Indigenous Peoples in Canada, and those eligible to work in\nCanada, will be given priority. Ontario Tech University respects people's different needs and therefore will take all reasonable steps to ensure accommodation for applicants where appropriate. The University is also committed to ensuring that confidentiality is maintained throughout all aspects of the recruitment cycle. If you require accommodation, please contact Julie Day , Health and Disability Management Specialist. For more information about the universities policies for accommodating employees with disabilities please review the university’s Accessibility Policy The university acknowledges the lands and people of the Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nation which is covered under the Williams Treaties. We are situated on the Traditional Territory of the Mississaugas, a branch of the greater Anishinaabeg Nation which includes Algonquin, Ojibway, Odawa and Pottawatomi. Job Location: Oshawa, Ontario, Canada Expected Start Date: 11/1/2026 Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://ontariotechu.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/2272?c=ontariotechu": {
      "title": "Manager, Student Housing",
      "description": "Manager, Student Housing | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Manager, Student Housing Oshawa, ON, Canada | req2272 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Manager, Student Housing Tracking Code: req2272 Faculty/Department: Office of the Deputy Provost Number of Positions: 1 Appointment Type: Full-Time Continuing Hours of Work: 35 hours per week Non-Union Hiring Zone: Job Level 10 - $91,413 - $114,266 Posting Date: October 14, 2026 Closing Date: October 27, 2026 (11:59 pm EST) Job Summary: The Manager, Student Housing, leads in the planning, delivery and assessment of programs and services that contribute to a holistic student housing strategy, which aligns with institutional priorities for student success, engagement, and well-being. The role fosters safe, inclusive, and supportive living environments while integrating Ontario Tech University's student housing partnerships with the university's broader student engagement and support strategies to deliver a cohesive, holistic student experience. Responsibilities/accountabilities: Partnership & Contract Management Provides oversight of residence partnerships and housing contracts to support Ontario Tech's student engagement and community-building objectives. Acts as the primary liaison with Campus Living Centres (CLC) and other housing partners, ensuring operational alignment, service quality, and accountability through ongoing monitoring of agreements, budgets, and performance metrics. Supports contract negotiations and partnership development, while collaborating with Legal and Finance to ensure compliance, fiscal stewardship, and effective risk management. Proactively identifies and resolves operational and student experience issues within residence facilities in partnership with CLC and other university stakeholders (Student Mental Health, Security, Campus Safety, etc.) Student Experience & Engagement Leads the development of an inclusive and engaging student housing experience portfolio that promotes belonging, equity, student leadership, and overall well-being. Collaborates with CLC, Student Engagement and Equity, the Student Union, Student Mental Health, Teaching and Learning, Careers, and other campus partners to design and deliver -programming that promotes positive social change and encourages an accessible, equitable, diverse, and inclusive campus culture. Establishes effective communication and referral pathways between residence life staff and campus services and oversees feedback mechanisms to drive continuous improvement. Provides guidance to residence staff in applying restorative practices, student conduct education, and proactive, early intervention approaches to support student success that are aligned with university strategies, policies and procedures. Operations & Oversight Provides insight to residence functions, including occupancy planning, room assignments, and billing processes, in collaboration with Campus Living Centres. Ensures equitable and appropriate housing accommodations for diverse student populations, including international students, varsity athletes, Indigenous learners, and students requiring accessibility supports. Contributes to campus crisis response and emergency management efforts in partnership with Security, Health & Safety, and housing partners, while promoting best practices in residence safety, community standards, and inclusive living environments. External & Community Relations Leads external and community engagement initiatives related to student housing by building partnerships with off-campus housing providers, municipal stakeholders, and local organizations to expand safe, affordable, and equitable options. Oversees Ontario Tech's off-campus housing programming, resources and supports, ensuring accessible and current information for students. Represents the university on local and provincial housing committees, advocating for student needs and sustainable housing policies, and maintains active relationships with housing professionals across Canada to share best practices and emerging trends. Strategic Leadership & Reporting Provides strategic leadership and data-informed reporting to support effective decision making in the student housing portfolio. Delivers regular updates on occupancy, student satisfaction, and financial performance to senior leadership, and contributes to the university's long term housing strategy, including capital planning, affordability initiatives, and demand forecasting. Leads or supports initiatives that advance accessibility, sustainability, and community engagement, while ensuring housing data, reporting, and assessment are aligned with institutional student experience frameworks and outcomes. Required Skills: Proven ability to build inclusive and student-centered communities. Exceptional communication, negotiation, and conflict resolution skills. Required Knowledge: Strong understanding of the Ontario housing market, post-secondary environment, and diverse student needs. Required Education: Completion of a University Program consisting of Four Academic Years Completion of an honours University degree program in a field of study directly related to the requirements of the job. An equivalent combination of education and related experience may be considered. * Verification of Academic credentials may be required Required Experience: Five to seven years of progressive experience or a combination of the following; student housing, residence life, property management, and/or student affairs leadership. Demonstrated experience managing third-party service contracts, budgets, and stakeholder relationships. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit in electronic format a covering letter and their resume. Applications will be accepted until October 27, 2026 or until a suitable candidate is found. We appreciate all applications received; however, only those candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. Ontario\nTech University is actively committed to equity, diversity, inclusion, indigenization\nand decolonization (EDIID), and welcomes applications from all qualified\ncandidates, while especially encouraging applications from First Nations,\nMetis, Inuit peoples, Indigenous peoples of North America, racialized persons,\npersons with disabilities, and those who identify as women and/or\n2SLGBTQ+.  All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply;\nhowever, Canadian citizens, permanent residents, Indigenous Peoples in Canada, and those eligible to work in\nCanada, will be given priority. Ontario Tech University respects people's different needs and therefore will take all reasonable steps to ensure accommodation for applicants where appropriate. The University is also committed to ensuring that confidentiality is maintained throughout all aspects of the recruitment cycle. If you require accommodation, please contact Julie Day , Health and Disability Management Specialist. For more information about the universities policies for accommodating employees with disabilities please review the university’s Accessibility Policy . The university acknowledges the lands and people of the Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nation which is covered under the Williams Treaties. We are situated on the Traditional Territory of the Mississaugas, a branch of the greater Anishinaabeg Nation which includes Algonquin, Ojibway, Odawa and Pottawatomi. Job Location: Oshawa, Ontario, Canada Expected Start Date: 12/7/2026 Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://ontariotechu.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/2271?c=ontariotechu": {
      "title": "Co-op and Internship Officer - Limited Term",
      "description": "Co-op and Internship Officer - Limited Term | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Co-op and Internship Officer - Limited Term Oshawa, ON, Canada | req2271 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/14/2026 Back to Search Co-op and Internship Officer - Limited Term Tracking Code: req2271 Faculty/Department: Co-op, Experiential Learning, and Career Development Office Number of Positions: 1 Appointment Type: Limited Term Hours of Work: 35 hours per week Salary Range: Level 8 - Starting Salary, Step 1 $80,379 Posting Date: October 14, 2026 Closing Date: November 3, 2026 (11:59 pm EST) Job Summary: The\nCo-op and Internship Officer will be primarily responsible for the development,\nimplementation, promotion and continued management of the co-op and internship\nprograms offered to Ontario Tech students, at both the undergraduate and\ngraduate levels. The Officer will connect students with co-op and internship\nopportunities, advise students of the program processes and procedures, provide\nongoing career development and employment support, and monitor students while on\nwork terms, while maintaining the learning outcomes as required by the university and\naccrediting bodies. The Officer will support employer partners through the recruitment, placement and evaluation\nprocesses, reinforcing Ontario Tech’s reputation as a partner of choice. Responsibilities/accountabilities: Program\nDesign and Development: Reviews overall program structure, philosophy and direction of co-op delivery. Monitors compliance with institutional policy and process as well as applicable governing bodies and legislation. Develops and updates as required, both regulations and procedures to maintain quality control. Works with the Registrar’s Office, setting eligibility criteria and expectations. Attends on-campus events and university program advisory meetings to build relationships with faculty and employers involved in each program. Maintains the quality of the program as a positive learning experience for the student and the employer. Program\nManagement and Delivery: Promotes programs to students and communicates program changes to students/stakeholders in coordination with the Registrar’s Office. Contributes to the development and updating of program policies and procedures where necessary and appropriate. Creates/updates promotional materials with Communications and Marketing (C+M). Coordinates student intake (obtain eligibility approval, etc.). Negotiates placement contracts and ensures quality of new placements; monitors safety of placement opportunities. Works in collaboration with Employer Relations and designated academic faculty to ensure the quality of placements/worksites, to verify positions meet program criteria. Works with Academic Advisors to approve student eligibility. Provides 1:1 student support re: co-op and internship program guidelines, eligibility, policies and processes. Coordinates job offer process. Resolves student and student/employer issues and supports student success through provision of regular feedback, coaching and 1:1 meetings. Coordinates with the Registrar’s Office to support students' transition between workplace and school and ensure smooth administration of co-op and internship program. Liaises with faculty members and academic advising to raise awareness of co-op and internship programs and opportunities (e.g. program mapping), as appropriate. Provides curricular input for and participates in the delivery of preparatory programming in collaboration with the Career Centre to ensure student readiness for the workforce. Supports and maintains institutional data as it relates to co-op and internship. Employer\nSupport: Co-develops plans to identify new and enhance current employer partnerships. Supports the coordination of employer visits to campus and recruitment/networking events. Arranges site visits/check-ins of students on work terms. Liaises with placement supervisors & employer HR departments. Monitors safety/risk management. Supports employers with funding sourcing, as appropriate. Maintains currency on labour market information and trends. Assessment\nand Evaluation: Communicates requirements and expectations for work‐term reports and employer evaluations. Facilitates completion and submission of student work term reports. Coordinates grading of student work term reports, with partnered faculty members. Facilitates completion of student‐employer work-term evaluations. Supports maintenance of program information system. Supports program evaluation and reporting to determine if programming is meeting student needs and generating the desired outcomes. Analyzes program data to inform evidence-based decisions on future programming. Supports the reporting and contributes towards satisfying the accreditation including data collection, surveys, and sample work/reports as required. Supports the provision of reports to Deans, Faculty and institution as it relates to co-op and Internship. Required Skills: Proficient in Microsoft Office Suite: Word, Excel, Publisher and Outlook, and willing to learn other computer software packages as required. Knowledge of web-based data management systems is preferred. Strong computer skills are essential. Excellent organizational and time management skills to deal with the high volume and interdisciplinary setting. Adaptability to changing priorities and working as part of a team and independently. Must have excellent communication (written and verbal), a proven record of past experience, and the ability to exercise tact and diplomacy when dealing with students, partners, university personnel and public. Excellent presentation skills. Excellent advising, problem-solving and customer service skills. Demonstrated ability to take initiative, problem solve, exercise sound judgment and respect the confidential nature of student information. Demonstrated sensitivity to the needs of students with a variety of abilities, cultures and backgrounds. Required Education: Completion of a\nuniversity program consisting of four academic years. An equivalent combination of education and related experience may be considered. * Verification of Academic credentials may be required Required Experience: Minimum 3 to 5 years of professional career development experience with demonstrated success in coordinating experiential learning programs such as co-ops or internships and providing career preparation in a post-secondary environment is required. Experience in designing, delivering and evaluating workshops and online modules. Previous experience in marketing, public presentations, project management and program administration. Sound knowledge of career development, hiring practices and employment trends. Knowledge of co-operative education and internship programs. Knowledge of the university’s academic programs and procedures preferred. How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit in electronic format a covering letter and their resume. Applications will be accepted until November 3, 2026 or until a suitable candidate is found. We appreciate all applications received; however, only those candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. This position falls within the bargaining unit represented by the Ontario Public Service Employees Union (OPSEU) and will be subject to the terms and conditions of the collective agreement between the university and the OPSEU. To the extent that policies are not included in the collective agreement, employment will also be governed by the university’s policies which may also be found on our website https://ontariotechu.ca and which may be amended from time-to-time. Ontario\nTech University is actively committed to equity, diversity, inclusion, indigenization\nand decolonization (EDIID), and welcomes applications from all qualified\ncandidates, while especially encouraging applications from First Nations,\nMetis, Inuit peoples, Indigenous peoples of North America, racialized persons,\npersons with disabilities, and those who identify as women and/or\n2SLGBTQ+. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply;\nhowever, Canadian citizens, permanent residents, Indigenous Peoples in Canada, and those eligible to work in\nCanada, will be given priority. Ontario Tech University respects people's different needs and therefore will take all reasonable steps to ensure accommodation for applicants where appropriate. The University is also committed to ensuring that confidentiality is maintained throughout all aspects of the recruitment cycle. If you require accommodation, please contact Julie Day , Health and Disability Management Specialist. For more information about the universities policies for accommodating employees with disabilities please review the university’s Accessibility Policy The university acknowledges the lands and people of the Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nation which is covered under the Williams Treaties. We are situated on the Traditional Territory of the Mississaugas, a branch of the greater Anishinaabeg Nation which includes Algonquin, Ojibway, Odawa and Pottawatomi. Job Location: Oshawa, Ontario, Canada Expected Start Date: 11/1/2026 Expected End Date: 11/1/2027 Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://ontariotechu.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/2268?c=ontariotechu": {
      "title": "IT Infrastructure Specialist",
      "description": "IT Infrastructure Specialist | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) IT Infrastructure Specialist Oshawa, ON, Canada | req2268 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/13/2026 Back to Search IT Infrastructure Specialist Tracking Code: req2268 Faculty/Department: Information Technology (IT) Services Department Number of Positions: 1 Appointment Type: Full-Time Continuing Hours of Work: 35 hours per week Salary Range: Level 9 - Starting Salary, Step 1 $90,525 Posting Date: October 13, 2026 Closing Date: November 2, 2026 (11:59 pm EST) Job Summary: This position supports the design, delivery, and ongoing improvement of\ntechnology solutions that enable university operations, reporting, research,\nand service delivery. The incumbent works across application development,\ndata/reporting, infrastructure support, and client consultation to help\ntranslate operational needs into reliable technical solutions. The role\nrequires a blend of software development, systems administration, database and\nreporting knowledge, and analytical problem-solving to support both day-to-day\noperations and longer-term technology planning. Through this work, the\nincumbent contributes to effective service delivery, informed decision-making,\nand the continued reliability and modernization of university technology\nservices. Responsibilities/accountabilities: 1. Researching, consulting and recommending suitable  technology for client needs Collaborate with users to understand their hardware needs and ensure they have the necessary tools throughout the hardware lifecycle. Understand short- and long-term technology trends. Ensure that the TELE program continues to benefit students, faculty, and staff at Ontario Tech. 2.  Proof of Concept and Piloting New Technologies Research, test, and implement new solutions to enhance the TELE program, including user group pilots. Explore alternative imaging solutions and software deployment systems, including cloud-based virtualization tools like Moka5, VMWare Horizon, and Citrix. 3.    DUO MFA Administrator Manage the DUO application Assist in establishing accounts for new users. Respond to Cherwell trouble tickets for end-user support Test and release updated versions of the DUO Mobile App 4.   System Management Conducted high-level system administration on server applications and installed critical patches and software upgrades with minimal user impact and downtime. Develop comprehensive documentation and provide expert instruction to users on system usage, supporting client devices on multiple platforms, including Windows, Mac, Linux, iOS, and Android. Remediate security risks identified to safeguard systems and data from external and internal threats. Execute backup strategies to minimize the risk of data loss and ensure the continuity of operations in case of a system failure or disaster. Collaborate with the procurement team to secure and maintain essential system licenses, ensuring the ongoing availability of critical software tools and services. Monitor system health proactively, troubleshoot issues promptly, and use technical expertise to resolve problems before they affect users. 5.   Development and Testing Design, develop, and maintain full-stack applications and databases for various university departments, including software and hardware requests and identity management reporting. Create and manage comprehensive test environments for internal and external testing, ensuring seamless deployment of new features with minimal downtime. Integrate web-based applications with enterprise systems, databases, and third-party applications, enhancing data insights and streamlining processes. Engineer complex system solutions to support institutional objectives, leveraging information architecture and process design to automate or enhance existing processes. 6.  Data Stewardship, Analysis and Reporting Administer and architect databases, optimize queries, and manage ETL processes for efficient data processing and analysis Design and maintain reporting applications, create customized reports, and automate data transfers to improve efficiency. Capture and analyze historical data for trend reporting, auditing, and accurate forecasting for budgeting and resource planning. Identify future needs, expand databases, and develop infrastructure budgets to meet evolving business requirements. Required Skills: A solid understanding of programming languages, databases, and software development frameworks. Familiarity with testing processes and methodologies is essential for ensuring the quality of the delivered software. This includes knowledge of test planning, test case development, and automated testing tools. Proactive about improving processes, identifying bottlenecks, and suggesting enhancements. Staying updated with industry trends and emerging technologies brings improvements to existing applications. Effective communication is vital for coordinating activities between various stakeholders, including developers, testers, product owners, and clients. Strong project management skills are crucial for overseeing IT projects. This includes defining project scope, setting objectives and timelines, resource planning, risk management, and ensuring timely delivery. Strong problem-solving abilities, analytical thinking, and attention to detail for troubleshooting developed applications and dashboards. Required Education: Completion of a university program consisting of four academic years An equivalent combination of education and related experience may be considered * Verification of Academic credentials may be required Required Experience: Over 3 years and including 5 years How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit in electronic format a covering letter and their resume. Applications will be accepted until November 2, 2026 or until a suitable candidate is found. We appreciate all applications received; however, only those candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. This position falls within the bargaining unit represented by the Ontario Public Service Employees Union (OPSEU) and will be subject to the terms and conditions of the collective agreement between the university and the OPSEU. To the extent that policies are not included in the collective agreement, employment will also be governed by the university’s policies which may also be found on our website https://ontariotechu.ca and which may be amended from time-to-time. Ontario\nTech University is actively committed to equity, diversity, inclusion, indigenization\nand decolonization (EDIID), and welcomes applications from all qualified\ncandidates, while especially encouraging applications from First Nations,\nMetis, Inuit peoples, Indigenous peoples of North America, racialized persons,\npersons with disabilities, and those who identify as women and/or\n2SLGBTQ+. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply;\nhowever, Canadian citizens, permanent residents, Indigenous Peoples in Canada, and those eligible to work in\nCanada, will be given priority. Ontario Tech University respects people's different needs and therefore will take all reasonable steps to ensure accommodation for applicants where appropriate. The University is also committed to ensuring that confidentiality is maintained throughout all aspects of the recruitment cycle. If you require accommodation, please contact Julie Day , Health and Disability Management Specialist. For more information about the universities policies for accommodating employees with disabilities please review the university’s Accessibility Policy The university acknowledges the lands and people of the Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nation which is covered under the Williams Treaties. We are situated on the Traditional Territory of the Mississaugas, a branch of the greater Anishinaabeg Nation which includes Algonquin, Ojibway, Odawa and Pottawatomi. Job Location: Oshawa, Ontario, Canada Expected Start Date: 11/23/2026 Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://ontariotechu.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/2256?c=ontariotechu": {
      "title": "Chief Invigilator - Limited Term",
      "description": "Chief Invigilator - Limited Term | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Chief Invigilator - Limited Term Oshawa, ON, Canada | req2256 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/6/2026 Back to Search Chief Invigilator - Limited Term Tracking Code: req2256 Faculty/Department: Office of the Deputy Provost Number of Positions: 2 Appointment Type: Limited Term Hourly Rate: $25.15/h Posting Date: October 6, 2026 Closing Date: October 20, 2026 (11:59 pm EST) Job Summary: Ontario Tech University is recruiting candidates to fill the\ncasual, part-time position of Chief Exam Invigilator in the Office of Student\nLife. Reporting to the Manager, Test Centre and Integrated Services, the\nincumbent is responsible for assisting with proctoring exams. The role ensures\nthat students registered with Student Accessibility Services receive their\napproved accommodations during examinations. Nature of work: The incumbent works in close collaboration with students, faculty and staff\nacross the university to support students with disabilities and utilizes\nhis/her experience and knowledge of the rights and responsibilities of\nindividuals with disabilities in Ontario under the Ontario Human Rights Code\nand the potential impact of disabilities on post-secondary participation and\nsuccess. Responsibilities/Accountabilities: Key\n     holders to the Test Centre which means opening or closing the Test Centre,\n     and ensuring that the Test Centre doors are locked when the Test Centre is\n     closed. Assist\n     with opening/setting up and closing/cleaning up of testing rooms and\n     ensure all hallways and emergency exits are cleared of personal belongings. Enforce\n     Test Centre protocol - secure exam files, complete appropriate paperwork\n     during each shift, maintain Test Centre security, and uphold a high level\n     of confidentiality. Check\n     students in and verify ID's, ensure academic integrity is maintained\n     throughout all exam activities, and monitor university students while they\n     are writing examinations and tests. Confirm\n     attendance of students, distribute examination materials to each seat\n     before students are permitted to enter the room, ensure that only\n     authorized items for use during the examination are at a student's desk. Refer\n     to the standard cover page sheet accompanying the exams for special\n     seating instructions, make sure students accommodated time are calculated\n     correctly and make sure students receive all their listed accommodations,\n     and report any anomalies immediately to the Test Centre coordinator. Responsible\n     for troubleshooting technical and exam related issues after regular\n     business hours and serve as back-up to the Test Centre Invigilators. Contact\n     the Test Centre coordinator or manager immediately if troubleshooting\n     efforts does not resolve incidents after business hours. Be\n     aware of exam anomalies, responsible for the completion and submission of\n     incident reports according to the university, Test Centre, and Student Accessibility\n     Services procedures and policies. File\n     completed exams with precision according to Test Centre protocols,\n     directing students and academic staff to the Test Centre, and assisting\n     with exam pick-ups,  completing after\n     hours exam pick-ups. Answer\n     student queries while remaining non-partisan and professional. Other\n     duties as assigned. Required Skills: Understanding\n     of legislative requirements in the area of human rights and accessibility. Good\n     judgment, decision making and problem-solving skills. Superior\n     organizational skills. Ability\n     to work effectively individually and within a team environment. Computer\n     word processing and data base skills. Collaborate\n     and communicate effectively in writing and orally. Proven\n     ability to carry out detailed procedures. Proven\n     ability to multi task and work well under pressure with strict deadlines. Demonstrated\n     ability to take initiative, problem solve and exercise sound judgment. Ability\n     to travel to and work out of two locations. Required Education: Completion\n     of post-secondary school program (college diploma or university degree) * Verification of Academic credentials may be required. Required Experience: Minimum\n     1 year of experience working in an accommodated Test Centre (preferred). Minimum\n     1 year of customer service experience (preferred). How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit in electronic format a covering letter and their resume. Applications will be accepted until October 20, 2026 or until a suitable candidate is found. We appreciate all applications received; however, only those candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. This position falls within the bargaining unit represented by the Public Service Alliance of Canada (PSAC) Teaching Assistant/Research Assistant/Invigilator and will be subject to the terms and conditions of the collective agreement between the university and the PSAC . To the extent that policies are not included in the collective agreement, employment will also be governed by the university’s policies which may also be found on our website https://ontariotechu.ca and which may be amended from time-to-time. Ontario\nTech University is actively committed to equity, diversity, inclusion, indigenization\nand decolonization (EDIID), and welcomes applications from all qualified\ncandidates, while especially encouraging applications from First Nations,\nMetis, Inuit peoples, Indigenous peoples of North America, racialized persons,\npersons with disabilities, and those who identify as women and/or\n2SLGBTQ+. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply;\nhowever, Canadian citizens, permanent residents, Indigenous Peoples in Canada, and those eligible to work in\nCanada, will be given priority. Ontario Tech University respects people's different needs and therefore will take all reasonable steps to ensure accommodation for applicants where appropriate. The University is also committed to ensuring that confidentiality is maintained throughout all aspects of the recruitment cycle. If you require accommodation, please contact Julie Day , Health and Disability Management Specialist. For more information about the universities policies for accommodating employees with disabilities please review the university’s Accessibility Policy The university acknowledges the lands and people of the Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nation which is covered under the Williams Treaties. We are situated on the Traditional Territory of the Mississaugas, a branch of the greater Anishinaabeg Nation which includes Algonquin, Ojibway, Odawa and Pottawatomi. Job Location: Oshawa, Ontario, Canada Expected Start Date: 11/2/2026 Expected End Date: 10/31/2027 Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://ontariotechu.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/4/home/requisition/2255?c=ontariotechu": {
      "title": "Scribe Invigilator - Limited Term",
      "description": "Scribe Invigilator - Limited Term | Job Details Sign In Create Profile English (US) Scribe Invigilator - Limited Term Oshawa, ON, Canada | req2255 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/6/2026 Back to Search Scribe Invigilator - Limited Term Tracking Code: req2255 Faculty/Department: Office of the Deputy Provost Number of Positions: 3 Appointment Type: Limited Term Hourly Rate: $25.15/h Posting Date: October 6, 2026 Closing Date: October 20, 2026 (11:59 pm EST) Job Summary: Ontario Tech University is seeking attentive individuals for\nthe casual, part-time position of Scribe Invigilator in the Office of the Deputy Provost. Reporting to the Manager, Test Centre and Integrated Services, Scribe\nInvigilators provide critical one-on-one support to students registered with\nStudent Accessibility Services, ensuring equitable access to exams by\ntranscribing responses and/or reading exam content aloud as per approved\naccommodations. We welcome applicants with academic backgrounds in Engineering,\nMath, Physics, Nursing, Health Sciences, and Computer Science, as many\nstudents receiving support write exams in these subject areas. Nature of work: Scribe Invigilators work in a quiet, individualized exam setting, providing\nsupport to students with accommodations. This role demands professionalism,\nneutrality, and strict adherence to accessibility, privacy, and academic\nintegrity protocols. Scribe Invigilators utilize their experience and knowledge\nof the rights and responsibilities of individuals with disabilities in Ontario\nunder the Ontario Human Rights Code. Responsibilities/Accountabilities: Writing\n     or typing responses exactly as dictated by the student, ensuring no\n     rewording or alterations are made. Reading\n     exam questions aloud if the student requires both reading and scribing\n     support, strictly following the printed text without rephrasing or\n     interpreting. Assisting\n     the student in navigating digital exam platforms if required, including\n     navigating between sections, handling software interfaces, or using\n     assistive technology. Requesting\n     clarification on capitalization, punctuation, and spelling, and allowing\n     the student to review and edit responses for accuracy. Adhering\n     strictly to protocols, maintaining neutrality, and refraining from\n     prompting or providing hints to the student. Maintaining\n     confidentiality of the exam content, as well as any personal or\n     accommodation information about the student, in alignment with Test Centre\n     and Student Accessibility Services' privacy standards. Arriving\n     on time to review protocols and prepare for any specific student\n     accommodations. Coordinating\n     closely with the Chief Invigilator to ensure that all accommodations and\n     exam protocols are consistently met. Assist\n     with the cleaning up of testing rooms. Be\n     aware of exam anomalies, and report incidents using the university, Test\n     Centre, and Student Accessibility. Services (SAS) procedures. Enforce\n     Test Centre protocol, complete appropriate paperwork during each shift,\n     maintain Test Centre security, and uphold a high level of confidentiality. Ensure\n     academic integrity is maintained throughout all exam activities. Other\n     duties as assigned. Required Skills: Understanding\n     of legislative requirements in the area of human rights and accessibility. Strong\n     listening and typing or handwriting skills. Excellent\n     verbal communication and clarity. Ability\n     to remain neutral, non-intrusive, and patient. Awareness\n     of accessibility principles and privacy/confidentiality standards. Comfort\n     with basic computer and exam software platforms. Ability\n     to follow protocols precisely and work one-on-one in a professional manner. Good\n     judgment, decision-making making and problem-solving skills. Superior\n     organizational skills. Ability\n     to work effectively individually and within a team environment. Computer\n     word processing and database skills. Collaborate\n     and communicate effectively in writing and orally. Proven\n     ability to carry out detailed procedures. Proven\n     ability to multitask and works well under pressure with strict deadlines. Demonstrated\n     ability to take initiative, problem solve and exercise sound judgment. Ability\n     to travel to and work out of two locations. Required Education: Completion\n     of post-secondary school program (college diploma or university degree) * Verification of Academic credentials may be required Required Experience: We\n     welcome applicants from diverse academic backgrounds. Familiarity with\n     subject areas such as engineering, mathematics, physics, nursing,\n     health sciences, or computer science is considered a strong asset, as\n     it will support students during discipline-specific exams. Minimum\n     1 year of experience working in an accommodated Test Centre (preferred) Minimum\n     1 year of customer service experience (preferred) How to Apply: Interested candidates should submit in electronic format a covering letter and their resume. Applications will be accepted until October 20, 2026 or until a suitable candidate is found. We appreciate all applications received; however, only those candidates selected for an interview will be contacted. This position falls within the bargaining unit represented by the Public Service Alliance of Canada (PSAC) Teaching Assistant/Research Assistant/Invigilator and will be subject to the terms and conditions of the collective agreement between the university and the PSAC . To the extent that policies are not included in the collective agreement, employment will also be governed by the university’s policies which may also be found on our website https://ontariotechu.ca and which may be amended from time-to-time. Ontario\nTech University is actively committed to equity, diversity, inclusion, indigenization\nand decolonization (EDIID), and welcomes applications from all qualified\ncandidates, while especially encouraging applications from First Nations,\nMetis, Inuit peoples, Indigenous peoples of North America, racialized persons,\npersons with disabilities, and those who identify as women and/or\n2SLGBTQ+. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply;\nhowever, Canadian citizens, permanent residents, Indigenous Peoples in Canada, and those eligible to work in\nCanada, will be given priority. Ontario Tech University respects people's different needs and therefore will take all reasonable steps to ensure accommodation for applicants where appropriate. The University is also committed to ensuring that confidentiality is maintained throughout all aspects of the recruitment cycle. If you require accommodation, please contact Julie Day , Health and Disability Management Specialist. For more information about the universities policies for accommodating employees with disabilities please review the university’s Accessibility Policy The university acknowledges the lands and people of the Mississaugas of Scugog Island First Nation which is covered under the Williams Treaties. We are situated on the Traditional Territory of the Mississaugas, a branch of the greater Anishinaabeg Nation which includes Algonquin, Ojibway, Odawa and Pottawatomi. Job Location: Oshawa, Ontario, Canada Expected Start Date: 11/2/2026 Expected End Date: 10/31/2027 Apply Now Share Save Job"
    }
  },
